Analogy is a learning method that deepens the understanding of new knowledge by comparing it with known knowledge. In the process of learning the Go language, I found that I can better understand the characteristics of the Go language through analogies with existing front-end knowledge.
## Syntax comparison
### import package method
The Go language's package import method is similar to the front-end module import method. Both are imported through the import keyword. However, the Go language's import method is more concise. You only need to write the package name, and no path is required.
```plain text
// js
import a from "a";
import b from "b"

// go
import (
    "a"
    "b"
)

```
### Variable declaration
```plain text
//js
var a = "a";
let b = "b";
const c = "c";

//go
var x string = "x";
x := "x" // Automatically fix variable types, declarations and assignments
```
### Conditions
```plain text
//js
if (a === "js") {

} else {

}

// go
if a == "go" {

} else {

}
```
### Loop
```plain text
// js
for(let i = 0; i < 100; i++) {

}

// go
for i := 0; i < 100; i++ {

}
```
### Function
```plain text
// js
function helloJS() {
    console.log("hello js");

    learnJS();
}

function learnJS() {
    console.log("learn js");
}

// go
func helloGo() {
    fmt.Println("hello go");
}

func learnGo() {
    fmt.Println("learn go");

    learnGo();
}
```
The similarities at the grammatical level have deepened my understanding of Go and made it easier for me to get started with Go.
